Z||000000||1FieroGTRacingInc||11-19-2000||09:35 PM||||Hey...   I found a second T-3 Garrett Turbo. Someone said that you were working on a *twin turbo*....   Do you plumb a turbo from each ex. manifold??  Is that what you're doing??  Have any advice??  Thank's  Steve||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shaun41178||11-20-2000||01:12 AM||||I know this is directed to Travis but I think I can provide some usefull info.<P>For a twin turbo setup you would want to run a turbo off of each bank of cylinders.  So that means you would have one for the 1,3,5 cylinders and then one for the 2,4,6 cylinders.  You would have a separate downpipe for each one which would then flow into a single exhaust containing the O2 sensor and then into a muffler or whatever you decide.  It might be a tight fit and depending on what tranny you have it could be easier or more difficult.  It seems that one has a bit more room in an auto tranny cause one does not have to worry about the shift cables.  <P>It is my opinion only but I think two separate T-3's are too large for only 3 cylinders each.  Low end torque would be close to stock in my opinion but once the rpms build, boost should come on fast.  The reason why I think the T-3's are too large is because they were designed for the 2.3 liter engines.  With a 2.8 engine it would be like a 1.4 liter on each cylinder bank.  Boost on the 2.3's come in at around 2500 or so rpm.  With a displacement of 1.4 being from each cylinder bank boost might not come in till about 4500 rpm or so and if you go all the way to 6000 rpm it might only create like 8 pounds of boost as the airflow just isnt there.  Now Perhaps with a 3.4 liter with a huge cam, turbo lag would be less of a problem but still would be there.  I geuss it is what you want.  Top end power or low end torque.  Even with 2 T-3's I don't think the V6 can really flow enough air at high rpm to get the turbos spinning fast enough to create the top end you want.  I would think a pair of T-25's would be better candidates for a twin turbo Fiero setup but that is just me.  Not trying to discourage you at all.  You can always go with the T-3's and if it doesn't give you the power you want you can always swap turbos.  Good luck if you decide to tackle this project.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>2.8 With nitrous<BR>Soon to have a 3.2 with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FieroGTRacingInc||11-20-2000||01:41 AM||||Thank's for the information, shaun....  This does sound like a fun(lol) project. I'm thinking about doing the twin's on a 3.4L.  How would it work on a *stock* 3.4l w/ all the fiero goodies*intake,etc.??  <BR>I just got through* today* pulling the 2.8L out of my totalled 87GT...  I pulled it outta the top..pretty easy outta the top too.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <BR> What did the T-25's come in??  Is that off of the 2.0L sunbird turbo?? I think it is..  I have a turbo off of a sunbird 2.0L...<P>WOW..THIS TWIN TALK IS GETTING ME EXCITED..  <P>                               Steve||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||11-20-2000||08:17 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> thanks shaun! yep hes right.. you'll have a pretty stiff lag time. running the twin on a 3.4 would be better but not by much. with the T3. look in he housings and what numbers can you see? it'll say A/R.68 look ion the exhaust side... running the 2 from a sunbird would be sweet... if you can get your hands on them. but the larger turbos will have better top end and will have a more pronounced boost curve (when the boost comes on your tires will be like fingernails on a chalk board looking for traction) where the smaller turbos will spool faster and be in the boost a less throttle. If i were you the bigger units and a 3.4 would be what i'd run with. I've had a few turbo cars and with their stock turbos they are in the boost just off idle and in traffic it gets annoying.. i've upgraded a 300zx TT and an 87 Supra Turbo with HKS' kits and their boost comes in about 3000+ which is nicer for daily driving and weekend tire thrashing. casue you don't have EVERYONE staring at you when your blow-off valve pops in traffic people tell you, you have a flat tire and that your cars leaking somthing... DUH i'ts leaking unneeded boost! strange people.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FieroGTRacingInc||11-21-2000||12:30 AM||||Thank's Travis...  So actually  which would be better... twin T-25's   or    a sigles T-3?? Using the 2.8, and using the 3.4??  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||11-21-2000||08:31 AM||||What do you want the car to do... be a drag car or a road race car? if your just looking for stop light bashing the smaller ones spool up faster for better low end torque, where the larger single will be better for a daily driver that you want to beat on, on the weekends. you'll be out of the boost in traffic! thats the biggest thing.. you won't have all the nasty heat eating away at everything under the hood. you'll just have heat when your deep in the throttle. I'm a fan of the 2.8... i Loved my SE.. STOOPID fast easy to make that way and easy to fix.. the 3.4 is the same but with the bigger motor you'll need a bigger turbo to support it. or use the T3 and have a little higher temps. How much HP/TQ are you looking for... what are the numbers on the side of the turbo you have? A/R.68 or .46??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">1.03!!! sorry..  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FieroGTRacingInc||11-21-2000||07:32 PM||||1fst2m6...  <BR>I want a stop light to........... stop light, for all the import's here...  How many HP??  I'm not sure....  lol<BR>I was going to ask you about those number's on the turbo's....   1 is...  AR.68  and the other one is...  AR.46..   What is the difference..  or is there a difference??  I'm doing this to a 86 SE!!  This is the first *notchback* that I've had...  hard to get used to...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  But I love it..  I can't wait to get it on the road.<BR>I found a 87 *BLUE* coupe today that had been wrecked in the front, The man want's $1,000  dor it though.  He said that it run's good.  121,000 miles.. and has a % speed in it, that would go in my 86 SE though, because it's an auto now.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> lol   HHHhhmmm  What do you think??||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaun41178||11-22-2000||05:09 AM||||The A/R ratio basically tells you the size of the turbo.  Typically speaking the larger the A/R ratio the larger the turbo.  The larger the turbo the larger the engine you will need to spin the turbo fast enough to make power.  All T-3 turbos are not the same.  They vary in the A/R ratio, trim size, exducer bore size(where the exhaust comes out the turbo) and little other things.  Some of the turbos that came on the Ford thunderbird 2.3's had an A/R ratio of .55.  According to Dennis Lagrua he says he is using a Turbo from a T-bird as well but that A/R is .63 according to him.  A guy here in Toledo has a Turbo thunderbird and he had the .55 ratio on his and he went to the j-yard and pulled a T-3 from a Mercury Merkur(same engine as the T-bird) and it was a .60 ratio.  That is the turbo that I have laying in my garage as my T-3 came from a Merkur as well.  Anyways the guy with the T-bird says he makes more power with the .60 turbo at less boost then the .55  did at more boost.  Explanation: the larger turbo is more efficient and is less of a exhaust restriction at boost then the smaller turbo.  Too small a turbo will give good low end torque but die top end like the T25 on the second generation DSm's and create lots of heat in the high rpms too.  A larger turbo will have more lag but when there is enough airflow to get it spinning fast enough to make boost then you better hold on cause it will get blurry fast in a hurry if you stay in it.<P>It is my personal opinion that a .60 A/R ratio is too small for a 2.8 V6 Fiero.  That is if you want top end.  Since you said you want that low end stop light match then I think a T-3 with a A/R of .55 or .60 would be good.  Boost will come in at around 2300 rpm or so and come on strong.  If I were to do a turbo and had no money concerns I would probably go with a A/R of around .65-.68 or a Grand National turbo probably.  These are expensive however right Travis?  I would prefer a larger turbo as lag doesn't bother me but top end would be amazing and it would also keep the heat down in the engine bay at high rpm.<P>The A/R ratio is probably on the intake side of the turbo somewhere and it will probably actually have (a/r .xx) written right on it making it simple.  That is how mine is anyways.<P>Since you are thinking about twin turbos here is a concept.  Get a .55 turbo and then get a .63 or a bit higher turbo.  Route the .55 off of one bank and the .63 off the other bank.  The .55 will help with the low end boost and the .63 will give the top end when the .55 is slacking off.  Sure they won't be sequential or if it will work right but if not then you could always drop the .60 and keep the .55 or vice versa.<P>I think you should reconsider the stoplight idea.  I myself find it very hard to get lined up at a light next to a car that wants to race and be at the front of the line at the same time.  Most of my races usually occur from a 40 mph roll and go up to about 80 or so.  A larger turbo like a .60 would do better in that situation.  I also find that if I do get a race with a car at a light we often get up to about 80 anyways before we shut down which is top end again. <P>Just my thoughts on the whole thing I guess<BR><P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>2.8 With nitrous<BR>Soon to have a 3.2 with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Performance</A>||||reg||1
